@page "/learn/environment-view"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Content for some environments only · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Content for some environments only</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You want a debug panel everywhere except Production. It runs in the browser, but the server renders it first. How does it find out which environment the app is in?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>IHostEnvironment</code> is now available in WebAssembly too, and
        <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> shows its content only in the environments you name. In .NET 10 you could
        check the environment on the server. A check that ran on both the server and in the browser didn't work.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Recent orders</i> page. Outside Production it shows a test-data banner and a small debug panel
        that names the environment. In Production it shows a warning instead. The page is static, but the debug panel runs on WebAssembly, in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        A WebAssembly component is rendered on the server first (prerendered), then starts again in the browser. This
        version of the panel asks <code>IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment</code> for the environment, the way .NET 10 apps
        did.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The page, with the .NET 10 debug panel" Url="demo/environment-view/orders/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Nothing to press: the page fails before anything reaches the browser. The server returns a 500, with no
            banner and no orders. The error names the panel's <code>Env</code> property, because the server has no
            <code>IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment</code> to give it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>No service worked in both places</h2>
    <p>The panel is rendered twice, first on the server and then in the browser, and each side registers a different service:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Who could answer "which environment?" in .NET 10</span>
        <pre class="snip">                          <span class="dim">server prerender</span>    <span class="dim">browser (WebAssembly)</span>
IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment   <span class="err">✗ not registered</span>    <span class="ok">✓</span>
IHostEnvironment              <span class="ok">✓</span>                   <span class="err">✗ not registered</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The panel asks for WebAssembly's service, so the server can't create the panel. Nothing catches the error, so
        the request fails with a 500. The orders and the banner never get sent.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Use the service both sides now have</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, WebAssembly registers <code>IHostEnvironment</code> too, with the server's environment name. No
        extra package is needed. Ask for that service instead, and the crash goes away. Then, to show the panel
        only in some environments, wrap it in <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> rather than writing an
        <code>if</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">@@* was: @@inject IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment Env *@@</span>
<span class="hl">@@inject IHostEnvironment Env</span>

<span class="hl">&lt;EnvironmentView Exclude="Production"&gt;</span>
    &lt;aside class="debug"&gt;Environment: @@Env.EnvironmentName&lt;/aside&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/EnvironmentView&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The <code>@@inject</code> line fixes the crash. <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> reads the same service
        itself, so a component that only needs the check can drop the <code>@@inject</code>. This panel keeps it to
        show the name. Here's the same page with the new panel.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, with EnvironmentView" Url="demo/environment-view/orders"
                Label=".NET 11: EnvironmentView">
        <Caption>
            Nothing to press: the fix is that the page loads. The banner and the debug panel both name the app's
            environment (<b>Development</b> locally, <b>Demo</b> on the public site). The panel's <b>Renderer</b> says
            WebAssembly: it's running in your browser, after the server prerendered it with the same service.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The fix is all in the panel, so here it is first. The page also uses <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code>, for its banner.</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Client/Environments/DebugPanel.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's panel, with the fix in full. The <code>@@inject</code> line stops the 500.
            <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> does the check.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/Environments/DebugPanelNet10.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's panel. The highlighted line causes the 500: the server can't provide that service. The property
            names differ too: WebAssembly's service has <code>Environment</code>; <code>IHostEnvironment</code> has
            <code>EnvironmentName</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Environments/RecentOrders.razor" Feature="environment-view">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page: the banner everywhere except Production, and the warning only in Production. The highlighted
            lines use the same tag but aren't part of the fix. This page runs on the server, where an <code>if</code> on
            <code>IHostEnvironment</code> already worked in .NET 10. The tag just makes it shorter.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Every option broke one side</h2>
    <p>For a component that renders on both sides, you had three options. None worked cleanly:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write it in .NET 10, and what happened</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@inject IWebAssemblyHostEnvironment Env</pre>
                <span class="why">the server can't provide it while prerendering</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ HTTP 500 for the whole page</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- + Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions --&gt;</span>
@@inject IHostEnvironment Env</pre>
                <span class="why">without the package, it won't compile in the client project (CS0234)</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ prerenders, then crashes when WebAssembly starts, and the Blazor error bar appears</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DebugPanel @@rendermode="new
    InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(
        <span class="err">prerender: false</span>)" /&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ no crash, but the panel only appears once WebAssembly has downloaded</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        A server-only component could inject <code>IHostEnvironment</code> and check it with an <code>if</code>. A
        component that rendered on both the server and in the browser needed two code paths, or a service of your own.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One service, and a tag on top</h2>
    <p>
        The second option now works, without the package: one <code>@@inject</code> line that both the server and the browser can supply.
        <code>&lt;EnvironmentView&gt;</code> replaces the <code>if</code>: list environments in <code>Include</code>
        or <code>Exclude</code>. Fig. 5 uses both.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It doesn't hide secrets.</b> The browser downloads a WebAssembly component's code in every environment, so anyone can see what's inside it. Keep secrets on the server.</li>
        <li><b>Separate names with commas.</b> <code>Include="Development,Staging"</code>. Case doesn't matter. If a name is in both lists, <code>Exclude</code> wins.</li>
        <li><b>It used to be called <code>EnvironmentBoundary</code>.</b> It was renamed in Preview 6, so older posts use the old name.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
